      "id": "p_734cda23",
      "title": "Eavesdropping Mitigation in RSMA Networks using Latent Space Modification in cGAN Models",
      "abstract": "Abstract The rate-splitting multiple access (RSMA) systems have redefined the way noise and interference is tackled in wireless networks. Nevertheless, RSMA technique is susceptible to eavesdropping due to free space wireless channel. The present research work aims to propose a mitigation against physical channel information intrusion where conditional generative adversarial network (cGANs) is utilized. In order to make the cGAN model more adaptive and robust, the cosine interpolation of latent space is proposed. More so, the latent space is populated by Gamma random variables which has the capability to represent the wireless fading channel more closely compared to Gaussian noise which is generally used in GAN architectures. An unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) is assumed to communicate with multiple users on the ground plane in the presence of eavesdroppers. The proposed cosine interpolated Gamma latent space cGAN (CIGLScGAN) deep learning model is used to mitigate the eavesdropping by generating a jamming signal. Further, this artificial jamming signal effects the intruding signal strength, however, generating a residual interference (RI) component which slightly lowers the overall performance. The results show a significant amount of improvement in the secrecy performance of the UAV-RSMA system model in terms of secrecy outage probability and secrecy capacity due to jamming caused by the CIGLScGAN trained model.",
