      "id": "p_043617bf",
      "title": "Gbps-rate sub-noise free-space optical communication using optical frequency comb diversity",
      "abstract": "High-security wireless communication is indispensable for mission-critical space-to-ground networks, unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) data relays, and maritime optical links, where physical link deployment is impractical and a low probability of detection (LPD) is essential. Sub-noise communication, which embeds information signals beneath the noise floor, provides a promising physical-layer security approach. However, existing sub-noise communication schemes are typically constrained by limited bandwidth or low transmission rates. Here, we propose a Gbps-rate free-space optical (FSO) sub-noise communication scheme enabled by optical frequency comb (OFC) diversity. By exploiting phase-coherent combining of a 40-line OFC, the proposed architecture extracts communication signals from noise-dominated observations and achieves a 15 dB processing gain. Numerical simulations demonstrate reliable recovery of a 10 Gbps carrier-suppressed double-sideband (CS-DSB) signal at an extreme input in-band SNR of -8 dB, satisfying the forward error correction (FEC) threshold of 10 −3 . Furthermore, Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence analysis supports the statistical covertness of the proposed scheme by revealing low distinguishability between signal-present and noise-only hypotheses. This work proposes and numerically validates an OFC-diversity-enabled communication architecture for Gbps-rate sub-noise free-space optical transmission under the adopted coherent reception assumptions, providing a scalable approach toward high-capacity LPD optical links.",
