      "id": "p_e9431b6a",
      "title": "Robust Beamforming for Physical-Layer Security in RIS-Assisted UAV Networks",
      "abstract": "The integration of unmanned aerial vehicles and reconfigurable intelligent surfaces constitutes a paradigm-shifting architecture for future wireless networks, particularly in scenarios requiring rapid deployment, extended coverage, and enhanced communication security. However, the inherent broadcast nature of wireless channels coupled with the exceptional line-of-sight propagation characteristics of unmanned aerial vehicles significantly exacerbates the vulnerability of transmitted data to malicious eavesdropping. To address these critical vulnerabilities, this paper proposes a comprehensive robust beamforming optimization framework designed specifically to maximize physical-layer security in reconfigurable intelligent surface-assisted unmanned aerial vehicle networks. A primary challenge in such dynamic environments is the acquisition of perfect channel state information. Therefore, we explicitly incorporate a bounded channel uncertainty model to account for imperfect channel state information at the transmitter regarding the eavesdropper channels. The overarching design objective is to maximize the worst-case secrecy rate of the legitimate communication link by jointly optimizing the active transmit beamforming vector at the unmanned aerial vehicle and the passive reflect beamforming matrix at the reconfigurable intelligent surface, subject to stringent transmit power constraints and unit-modulus phase shift constraints. Given the highly non-convex nature of the formulated max-min optimization problem, we develop an efficient alternating optimization algorithm leveraging semidefinite relaxation, successive convex approximation, and the S-procedure to ensure mathematical tractability and convergence. Extensive numerical simulations demonstrate that the proposed robust design substantially outperforms conventional non-robust schemes and effectively mitigates the adverse impacts of channel state information uncertainties, thereby guaranteeing resilient and secure wireless communications in highly dynamic adversarial environments.",
